WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.072
This briefing comes from Book 15 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.072 --> 00:00:07.874
Compliance Automation
and Day-Two Operations.

4
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:11.430
It's written for CIOs, service
desk and operations leads,

5
00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.213
and information system security officers.

6
00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:15.626
I'll take four questions.

7
00:00:15.626 --> 00:00:19.138
Why does modernization succeed
or fail at the help desk?

8
00:00:19.138 --> 00:00:20.724
What does day two run on?

9
00:00:20.724 --> 00:00:21.637
Who runs it?

10
00:00:21.637 --> 00:00:23.900
And how do you run it, and prove it works?

11
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.450
The short version is this.

12
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.150
Cutover proves the new
estate can be built.

13
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:32.150
The first locked-out user on Monday
morning proves whether it can be run.

14
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:35.530
Let's start with why the
help desk holds the keys.

15
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.681
Modernization programs spend most
of their attention on day one:

16
00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:47.267
the tenant is built, identities
sync, the cutover runs.

17
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:51.039
But the estate is judged on
day two, when every reset,

18
00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:54.092
unlock and new hire
lands at the help desk.

19
00:00:54.092 --> 00:00:57.143
In Entra ID the help desk's power is real.

20
00:00:57.143 --> 00:01:00.756
Microsoft calls the Helpdesk
Administrator role privileged,

21
00:01:00.756 --> 00:01:05.481
and warns that changing a user's password
can mean taking over that user's identity.

22
00:01:05.481 --> 00:01:09.298
Forcing MFA re-registration
wipes the user's methods,

23
00:01:09.298 --> 00:01:12.855
and whoever registers next
controls the account.

24
00:01:12.855 --> 00:01:17.080
So a reset on an unverified
request isn't a support task.

25
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.580
It's handing the account to whoever asked.

26
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.599
Attackers know this; CISA
has described groups

27
00:01:22.599 --> 00:01:25.468
that talked help desk
staff into exactly that.

28
00:01:25.468 --> 00:01:29.067
And when the governed path is
hard, the desk works around it.

29
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:32.330
Next, what federal guidance
says about recovery.

30
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.655
Two federal documents frame
the reset and recovery path.

31
00:01:39.655 --> 00:01:45.291
OMB's zero trust memo requires
phishing-resistant MFA, and then names the hole:

32
00:01:45.291 --> 00:01:48.374
recovery is a potential
bypass of all that,

33
00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:51.192
and a serious threat if it isn't handled.

34
00:01:51.192 --> 00:01:55.258
It asks for high-friction methods
that are costly for an attacker,

35
00:01:55.258 --> 00:01:57.442
like in-person checks or live video.

36
00:01:57.442 --> 00:02:01.631
NIST's digital identity guidelines
give recovery its own section.

37
00:02:01.631 --> 00:02:06.472
They allow a help desk agent only on
the basis of a documented risk analysis,

38
00:02:06.472 --> 00:02:09.362
repeated proofing has
to match the original,

39
00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:12.642
and every recovery must
notify the account holder.

40
00:02:12.642 --> 00:02:17.366
Put together, the help desk reset should
be the exception, not the main path,

41
00:02:17.366 --> 00:02:22.167
at least as strong as the original
proofing, and always followed by a notice.

42
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:26.005
Email, caller ID and knowledge
questions verify no one.

43
00:02:26.005 --> 00:02:29.030
Now look at the same problem
from the compliance side.

44
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.255
An authorization describes
the system as it was assessed.

45
00:02:36.255 --> 00:02:41.310
The day after, the help desk resets
passwords, onboards people, changes groups

46
00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.892
and fixes a sync error, and every one
of those changes touches a control.

47
00:02:45.892 --> 00:02:50.220
Done as portal clicks, they leave
no record the control recognizes,

48
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.580
so the package ages with every click.

49
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.490
The baseline only asks for
automation in specific places,

50
00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:00.155
so Book 15's push for automation
is really about volume.

51
00:03:00.155 --> 00:03:04.416
At day-two volume, people assembling
the record by hand can't keep up,

52
00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:07.980
and what they produce is a
reconstruction, not evidence.

53
00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.340
So a control has three layers:
designed, deployed as code,

54
00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:17.080
and operated, with an owner, a
gate, a clock and an evidence feed.

55
00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:20.624
Every operation should start
from the source of truth,

56
00:03:20.624 --> 00:03:24.405
carry its approval, close with
proof and emit its evidence.

57
00:03:24.405 --> 00:03:27.030
Miss one, and the work can't be attested.

58
00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.780
Let's look at the front door.

59
00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.720
Day two starts at the front
door, and the rule is that

60
00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.517
nothing is handled as ad hoc
email to the cloud services team.

61
00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:44.489
Self-service requests, the team mailbox,
the governance platform and monitoring

62
00:03:44.489 --> 00:03:48.867
alerts all land in one triage queue
in the service management platform.

63
00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:53.830
The mailbox rule has to redirect, not
copy, or you end up with two queues.

64
00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:58.280
Triage then asks one question: what
authority does this task need?

65
00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.225
The book sorts requests into classes,
from no access change at all,

66
00:04:02.225 --> 00:04:05.294
through changes the
requester's manager approves,

67
00:04:05.294 --> 00:04:09.605
up to governance actions like admin
consent, which are never automated.

68
00:04:09.605 --> 00:04:14.195
The checks run in a fixed order, so the
dangerous cases get caught first, such

69
00:04:14.195 --> 00:04:19.155
as anything claiming to come from Microsoft,
or a target holding a directory role.

70
00:04:19.155 --> 00:04:23.580
And an approval nobody answers
expires to reject, never to approve.

71
00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.080
Next, roles.

72
00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:32.456
In Entra ID, what a technician
can do depends on three things:

73
00:04:32.456 --> 00:04:36.525
the role decides the action, the
target decides whether it applies,

74
00:04:36.525 --> 00:04:39.855
and the administrative unit
narrows who it applies to.

75
00:04:39.855 --> 00:04:43.889
The program gives each task its
least-privileged role, and keeps the

76
00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:48.355
role that can reset privileged
administrators outside the desk entirely.

77
00:04:48.355 --> 00:04:50.518
But capability isn't authority.

78
00:04:50.518 --> 00:04:53.836
Some built-in roles can do
far more than the task needs,

79
00:04:53.836 --> 00:04:57.380
so the program narrows them,
sometimes with a custom role.

80
00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.455
Executives sit in restricted units
the general desk can't reach,

81
00:05:01.455 --> 00:05:04.530
and roles are eligible
through PIM, not standing.

82
00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.155
And approval isn't identity verification.

83
00:05:07.155 --> 00:05:10.274
The verification ladder
is the CISO's decision:

84
00:05:10.274 --> 00:05:15.655
a phishing-resistant sign-in first, and
identity proofing when every method is lost.

85
00:05:15.655 --> 00:05:19.706
Then a short-lived one-time pass,
a notice on a separate channel,

86
00:05:19.706 --> 00:05:22.168
and two approvers for high-risk tasks.

87
00:05:22.168 --> 00:05:23.820
Now, the catalog.

88
00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.608
Every routine change
arrives as a typed request.

89
00:05:30.608 --> 00:05:36.022
The help desk catalog covers joiners,
movers, leavers, credentials, groups,

90
00:05:36.022 --> 00:05:38.242
licenses, guests and a few more,

91
00:05:38.242 --> 00:05:43.657
each tied to the control it exercises
and the approval that control needs.

92
00:05:43.657 --> 00:05:48.157
And each item closes only when a read
of the target confirms the change.

93
00:05:48.157 --> 00:05:52.587
The leaver fails audits most often,
because it means disabling the account,

94
00:05:52.587 --> 00:05:55.945
revoking sessions, deprovisioning
every connected app,

95
00:05:55.945 --> 00:05:58.232
and reassigning what the person owned.

96
00:05:58.232 --> 00:06:00.969
SaaS integration is the riskiest moment.

97
00:06:00.969 --> 00:06:05.706
In GCC, an administrator can federate
a third-party service in about fifteen

98
00:06:05.706 --> 00:06:08.563
minutes, and nothing in
that path asks whether

99
00:06:08.563 --> 00:06:11.495
the service is authorized
to hold federal data.

100
00:06:11.495 --> 00:06:17.011
So the catalog records a FedRAMP Marketplace
verdict before anything is configured,

101
00:06:17.011 --> 00:06:20.507
with Class C or higher as the
default for identity data.

102
00:06:20.507 --> 00:06:23.930
Next, the inventory everything routes to.

103
00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.579
Every ticket names an asset,

104
00:06:29.579 --> 00:06:33.955
so the help desk can only be as
right as the inventory it routes to.

105
00:06:33.955 --> 00:06:37.615
The configuration database
earns that trust by agreeing

106
00:06:37.615 --> 00:06:40.742
with one authoritative
identity, not by being one.

107
00:06:40.742 --> 00:06:45.381
Discovery fills it in; the naming and
addressing record governs identity.

108
00:06:45.381 --> 00:06:50.542
Where the two disagree, the database is
wrong, and the disagreement is a finding.

109
00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:53.287
ServiceNow already prevents duplicates and

110
00:06:53.287 --> 00:06:56.030
controls which source
writes each attribute.

111
00:06:56.030 --> 00:07:01.494
The program adds one pass: match each
asset to the naming record, bind it, and

112
00:07:01.494 --> 00:07:07.317
flag the rest, either a shadow asset nobody
approved or a gap to fix at the source.

113
00:07:07.317 --> 00:07:09.867
Duplicates are resolved by binding,

114
00:07:09.867 --> 00:07:13.180
not deleting, so no open
finding loses its asset.

115
00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:18.068
Otherwise a fix can land on one copy
while the finding stays open on the other.

116
00:07:18.068 --> 00:07:21.550
Next, automating the
controls in each cloud.

117
00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.894
Microsoft, AWS and VMware
already ship tools that

118
00:07:28.894 --> 00:07:32.400
test most of the settings
an assessor asks about.

119
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.576
The gap is turning each failed
test into owned work with a clock,

120
00:07:36.576 --> 00:07:38.825
and each closed task into a record.

121
00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:44.631
So the program runs one loop everywhere:
the native tool tests, drift is detected,

122
00:07:44.631 --> 00:07:47.534
a task is raised, the
owning team fixes it,

123
00:07:47.534 --> 00:07:50.525
and the task closes on
a re-test, not a click.

124
00:07:50.525 --> 00:07:54.762
The service management platform reads
control state; it never writes it.

125
00:07:54.762 --> 00:07:58.592
But the government clouds differ,
and several tools are missing,

126
00:07:58.592 --> 00:08:00.687
still rolling out, or being retired.

127
00:08:00.687 --> 00:08:05.657
The one with a firm deadline
is AWS's ServiceNow connector,

128
00:08:05.657 --> 00:08:08.763
which loses support on March 31, 2027.

129
00:08:08.763 --> 00:08:10.438
The slide lists the rest.

130
00:08:10.438 --> 00:08:15.225
And on premises, the agency produces
every piece of evidence itself.

131
00:08:15.225 --> 00:08:19.190
Before building anything, look
at what ServiceNow already does.

132
00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:27.665
Before anyone builds a compliance application,
know what the platform already does.

133
00:08:27.665 --> 00:08:32.012
ServiceNow's native compliance
applications schedule control tests,

134
00:08:32.012 --> 00:08:36.285
turn gaps into issues, walk the steps
of the Risk Management Framework,

135
00:08:36.285 --> 00:08:38.495
give one view of every plan of action,

136
00:08:38.495 --> 00:08:42.253
and exchange security plans and
assessment results in OSCAL.

137
00:08:42.253 --> 00:08:45.815
The government offering is listed
as FedRAMP Certified at High.

138
00:08:45.815 --> 00:08:49.715
So the recommendation is to buy
the native applications first,

139
00:08:49.715 --> 00:08:51.665
and build only what they don't do:

140
00:08:51.665 --> 00:08:56.840
join every finding to the authoritative
asset identity, close only with proof, flag

141
00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.865
human-only closures, and check the platform's
control set against the control map.

142
00:09:01.865 --> 00:09:07.309
The principle underneath is this: a control
is proven by system evidence wherever

143
00:09:07.309 --> 00:09:11.953
a system can see it, and people attest
only to what no system can observe.

144
00:09:11.953 --> 00:09:15.530
Next, who decides, and
who holds the keyboard.

145
00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.918
Day two is run by the people who touch
the estate most and decide the least.

146
00:09:23.918 --> 00:09:28.007
So every decision sits with someone
who isn't holding the keyboard,

147
00:09:28.007 --> 00:09:31.056
and every artifact has one
owner and a review date.

148
00:09:31.056 --> 00:09:34.503
The service desk takes every
request, classifies it,

149
00:09:34.503 --> 00:09:38.592
verifies the person and executes
against an approved ticket.

150
00:09:38.592 --> 00:09:42.405
Cloud services is the escalation
tier, not a second queue.

151
00:09:42.405 --> 00:09:47.480
Technicians approve nothing, and a supervisor
never approves a ticket they execute.

152
00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.967
Platform owners own the runbooks.

153
00:09:49.967 --> 00:09:52.443
The control owner owns the approval gate.

154
00:09:52.443 --> 00:09:56.102
The service management team
builds both and decides neither;

155
00:09:56.102 --> 00:10:00.479
changing an approval rule is itself
a change the change board authorizes.

156
00:10:00.479 --> 00:10:03.965
The board authorizes changes
but doesn't accept risk;

157
00:10:03.965 --> 00:10:07.292
that goes to the CISO or
the authorizing official.

158
00:10:07.292 --> 00:10:10.605
A contracted desk fits,
because contractors prepare

159
00:10:10.605 --> 00:10:13.617
and execute while government
officials decide.

160
00:10:13.617 --> 00:10:15.500
Now, the runbook.

161
00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:24.287
The runbook turns all of this into a
rhythm, and each step says what it proves.

162
00:10:24.287 --> 00:10:29.502
Daily, a read-only sync probe replaces the
habit of logging on to the sync servers

163
00:10:29.502 --> 00:10:32.108
each morning, every error becomes a task,

164
00:10:32.108 --> 00:10:35.663
and a check confirms every
log source is still sending.

165
00:10:35.663 --> 00:10:40.198
Weekly, detection rules are tested
against replayed known-bad events,

166
00:10:40.198 --> 00:10:44.500
and any task closed without proof
is either evidenced or reopened.

167
00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:48.475
Monthly and quarterly reviews
follow, and the slide lists them.

168
00:10:48.475 --> 00:10:51.550
The runbook also rewrites everyday tasks.

169
00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:55.325
A reset password goes out on a
channel separate from the request.

170
00:10:55.325 --> 00:11:00.887
And a requester's script is never pasted
and run: parameters travel, commands don't.

171
00:11:00.887 --> 00:11:04.026
Finally, harnesses check
the automation itself.

172
00:11:04.026 --> 00:11:07.054
The catch is that a harness
that stops running looks

173
00:11:07.054 --> 00:11:10.012
just like one that passes,
so check that each one ran.

174
00:11:10.012 --> 00:11:12.230
That brings us to the decisions.

175
00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.355
So, four decisions.

176
00:11:17.355 --> 00:11:20.099
Put identity verification under the CISO,

177
00:11:20.099 --> 00:11:23.910
and treat every authenticator
reset as an account transfer,

178
00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:29.017
with the most friction on the most sensitive
accounts and an alert on every reset.

179
00:11:29.017 --> 00:11:32.256
Build one front door:
redirect the team mailbox,

180
00:11:32.256 --> 00:11:36.906
triage by authority, and let
unanswered approvals expire to reject.

181
00:11:36.906 --> 00:11:41.732
Buy the platform's native compliance
applications first, record an authorization

182
00:11:41.732 --> 00:11:46.280
verdict before any SaaS integration, and
check every feature in your own cloud.

183
00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.120
And fund the third
layer: an owner, a gate,

184
00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:52.493
a clock and an evidence
feed for every control.

185
00:11:52.493 --> 00:11:54.692
The next step needs no procurement.

186
00:11:54.692 --> 00:12:00.304
Measure before you build, with a read-only
inventory and sync probe in a test

187
00:12:00.304 --> 00:12:06.005
tenant, and plan the move off AWS's
ServiceNow connector before March 31, 2027.

188
00:12:06.005 --> 00:12:10.705
Operations is where modernization
becomes routine or quietly reverts.

189
00:12:10.705 --> 00:12:14.430
Book 15 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

190
00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.260
Thank you.
